{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q014_paper_example_reclassification_pa1492","answer":"In SCOP 1.69, PA1492 and nucleoside 2-deoxyribosyltransferase shared only class c, “Alpha and beta proteins (a/b)” (SUNID 51349). PA1492 domain d1t1ja_ (SUNID 106249) occupied its own fold c.137, superfamily c.137.1, and family c.137.1.1, whereas 1f8y domain d1f8ya_ (SUNID 31395) belonged to the flavodoxin-like fold c.23, N-deoxyribosyltransferase superfamily c.23.14, and family c.23.14.1. Thus, SCOP 1.69 asserted neither a shared fold nor superfamily-level common ancestry; this does not prove that they were nonhomologous or independently evolved.

The current endpoint identifies SCOPe 2.08 as the current stable release. There, PA1492’s principal domain is d1t1ja1, residues 1–118 (the same public SUNID 106249), classified as c.23.14.2. It now shares the flavodoxin-like fold c.23 and N-(deoxy)ribosyltransferase-like superfamily c.23.14 (SUNID 52309) with d1f8ya_, so SCOPe now supports probable common ancestry. They remain in different families: PA1492 family c.23.14.2 (SUNID 110461) versus N-deoxyribosyltransferase family c.23.14.1 (SUNID 52310). Both chains in each PDB have the corresponding classification.

Accordingly, the relationship changed from broad class-level similarity only to a shared, homology-bearing superfamily, not to family-level identity. The placement does not justify transferring the exact nucleoside 2-deoxyribosyltransferase reaction, substrate or ligand specificity, oligomeric behavior, whole-protein function, or assuming interchangeable structural templates to PA1492. In particular, 1f8y’s ligand-complex annotation is not evidence that PA1492 binds the same ligand. The retrieved API establishes the before/after placement but not the historical rationale attributed to Andreeva et al.; verifying that rationale would require the paper and supporting global structural alignment, core-sequence/active-site correspondence, while exact function transfer would additionally require ligand-bound and biochemical evidence.","evidence_used":["scop-homology/SKILL.md","scop-homology/references/biological-interpretation.md","scop-homology/references/api-usage.md","scop-homology/scripts/scop_compare.py","/api/v1/releases/current","/api/v1/releases/1.69/pdb/1t1j/domains","/api/v1/releases/1.69/pdb/1f8y/domains","/api/v1/releases/2.08/pdb/1t1j/domains","/api/v1/releases/2.08/pdb/1f8y/domains","/api/v1/releases/1.69/sids/d1t1ja_/parents","/api/v1/releases/1.69/sids/d1t1ja_/annotations","/api/v1/releases/1.69/sids/d1t1ja_/homology","/api/v1/releases/1.69/sids/d1f8ya_/parents","/api/v1/releases/1.69/sids/d1f8ya_/annotations","/api/v1/releases/1.69/sids/d1f8ya_/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1t1ja1/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1t1ja1/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1t1ja1/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1f8ya_/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1f8ya_/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1f8ya_/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/52171/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/52171/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/52309/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/52309/homology"]}]}
